[Fact:spectrumcomputing:Spectrum Computing]
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/5933"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">After Shock</a>
(credits, magazine scores and the Sinclair User solution).

[Fact:hiatus:The Comeback]
After Shock came after a year-long gap in Interceptor's releases, and cost
&pound;9.95 against the &pound;5.50 of the earlier games. Crash noted that
the inlay credits Robin Chapman for the graphics while the loading screen
credits Terry Greer, and that text no longer scrolls off the top of the
screen. See the <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=5933"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:take:Take, Not Get]
Sinclair User and Your Sinclair both complained that the game knows TAKE
but not GET ("GET PEN. 'Try another command.'"). The vocabulary at #R$B0ED
bears this out: command #N$0E is TAKE with no synonym.
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=5933"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Reviews</a>

[Fact:grammar:Grammar Matters]
The instructions explain that the parser deliberately accepts only
grammatical commands. ZX Computing found it insisted on exact phrases such as
BRACE STAIRS WITH BEAM. See the <a href="https://idpixel.ru/games/a/aftershock/files/AfterShock___Instructions.txt"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">instructions</a> and the
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=5933"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:animated:Moving Pictures]
Some pictures are animated. Computer &amp; Video Games and Your Computer
mention a hand under the rubble that opens and closes, and an oil tanker
gushing oil. See the <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=5933"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:scores:Reviews]
Crash issue 34 gave it 89%, Sinclair User issue 56 5/5, Your Sinclair
issue 12 7/10 and Your Computer 3/5, and ZX Computing gave it a Globert
award. Sinclair User printed a four-part solution in issues 110 to 113 in
1991. Source: <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/5933"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:leftovers:Leftovers]
The routine at #R$A164 divides #REGhl by four, but nothing calls it. The
first entry of the jump table at #R$A779 is unused, as are the first two slots
of the room description table at #R$AE7D, for rooms #N$00 and #N$01
(#N$AE7D and #N$AE7F).

[Fact:engine:The Interceptor Engine]
After Shock is built on the same engine as the earlier Interceptor games.
Its routines are cross-referenced to Warlord, The Jewels Of Babylon, Heroes
Of Karn, Forest At World's End and Message From Andromeda throughout this
disassembly.

[Fact:swearing:A Rude Word]
Token 39 (#N$27) in the vocabulary, at #R$B0ED(#N$B1F2), is the single word
"FUCK". The game does recognise it as a verb: it is listed in the verb table
at #R$B420(#N$B447), which leads to the script at #R$BD2E(#N$C87E). Swear at
your body or at a woman and the game says "You are a sick person."
(#R$FEC5); swear at anything else and it says "That is not advisable."
(#R$FED1). Leave out what you are swearing at and it says
"Please be more specific." (#R$CDC1), and if it doesn't know the word it says
"Please rephrase that." (#R$CDD1).
